(Bug #58's argument half. Arguments are passed by the runner, so unlike the
 environment cases these can be checked against exact bytes rather than
 against a control. The declaration says buffer; the initializer is an
 argument read; before the fix the name was re-labelled text, so `Print`
 rendered the buffer header as a C string - an empty line - and `size`
 answered -1 from the file fallback.

 The last block is `Set` rather than a declaration, on a buffer that was
 declared with a size, and it was hit too - `Set` on a name with no type of
 its own routes through the same arm. Its old output is the one that reads
 most innocently: `Print` was right and only `size` was wrong, because the
 name had become a pointer to the argument text itself. Test 366 follows
 that pointer to where it does real damage.)

a buffer called firstarg is arguments's first.
Print firstarg's type.
Print firstarg.
Print firstarg's size.
Print firstarg's empty.

a buffer called lastarg is arguments's last.
Print lastarg.
Print lastarg's size.

a buffer called programname is arguments's name.
Print programname's type.
Print programname's empty.

a buffer called assigned is 64 bytes in size.
Set assigned to arguments's first.
Print assigned's type.
Print assigned.
Print assigned's size.
